 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Dielectric",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Temperature characteristic 5C is the EIA class C0G: the capacitance moves by 0 ±30 ppm/℃ between 25 and 125 ℃, referred to 25 ℃. Below the reference temperature the allowed change is in Table A. Over temperature cycling the drift stays within ±0.2% or ±0.05 pF, whichever is the larger."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Rated voltage",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "50 V DC is the most that may sit across the part continuously. With an AC component riding on DC, the zero-to-peak value must stay at or below 50 V; with pure AC or pulses the peak-to-peak value must. Surges, ESD and pulse spikes are held to the same limit. Overvoltage can break down the dielectric layers and short the part, after a time that depends on the voltage and the ambient temperature."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Self-heating",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "AC or pulse current through the part heats it through dielectric loss. For parts rated below 100 VDC the load is limited so the body rises less than 20 °C above a 25 °C ambient, and the surface temperature including self-heating stays within the 125 ℃ upper operating limit."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Voltage, temperature and aging effects",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "High dielectric constant types (X7R, X5R and the like) lose capacitance with DC bias, change with AC level and temperature, and age downward over time. Over about 3000 h C0G shows no aging change, while X7R falls by about 4% and X5R by about 9%."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Keep reels at +5 to +40 ℃ and 20 to 70 %RH, preferably under 30 ℃, away from direct sun, condensation and corrosive gases such as hydrogen sulfide, sulfur dioxide, chlorine and ammonia, which attack the terminations. Long or hot, damp storage degrades packaging and oxidises the electrodes: after six months check the packaging and mounting, after a year the solderability too. Capacitance of long-stored parts is measured after a heat treatment."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Mounting position",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Orient the chip so that board bending stress runs along its length rather than across it, keep it away from board separation lines and screw holes, and where it must sit near a separation line add slits there. Parts removed from equipment are not reused, and Sn-Zn solders reduce MLCC reliability."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Placement machine",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Set the pickup nozzle's lowest point so it does not flex the board, back the board with support pins, and keep the nozzle, suction path and locating claws clean and replaced on schedule; worn or dirty parts press unevenly and crack chips."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reflow soldering",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Preheat board and parts so the step from preheat to solder temperature, ΔT, is within 190 ℃ for the 21 size, and keep the same ΔT between a hot assembly and any cleaning solvent. Peak 240 to 260 ℃ in air or nitrogen with Sn-3.0Ag-0.5Cu. Repeated passes add up against the allowed time-at-temperature. Too low a peak risks poor wetting, voids, whiskers, weak joints, poor self-alignment and tombstoning; too much paste makes a tall fillet that passes stress into the chip, too little leaves a weak joint."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Flow soldering",
   "page": 17,
   "text": "Flow soldering is allowed only for the 155, 18, 21 and 31 sizes, with ΔT up to 150 ℃, preheat peak 100 to 120 ℃ and solder peak 250 to 260 ℃. Long or hot dwell leaches the terminations and can lower capacitance. The fillet should not climb above the chip's thickness. Parts held by adhesive before flow use an adhesive that is non-corrosive, insulating, halogen free and strong when wet, applied in a controlled dot under the chip body; flux is thin, with halide at most 0.1%, and termination leaching is held under 25% of the end area and of the edge length A-B."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Rework",
   "page": 18,
   "text": "Preheat to at least 150 ℃ and keep the iron at 350 ℃ max. for the 21 size, finishing quickly and letting the board cool slowly. Use an iron tip of ø3 mm or less that does not touch the chip, and solder wire of ø0.5 mm or less. A spot heater is gentler: nozzle at least 5 mm away, aimed at 45°, air at 400 °C max., for under 10 s on chips up to 3.2 × 1.6 mm."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Board handling and separation",
   "page": 19,
   "text": "Bending or twisting a loaded board, test probes without backing pins, cleaning resonance, dropping, insertion of leaded parts, connectors and screws can all crack chips, which then lose insulation resistance and may short. Hold boards by both edges, support them under probes and insertions, and use a torque driver. A router separator stresses the board least; with a jig, grip close to the jig and bend toward the component side; a disc separator needs aligned blades and a properly cut V-groove."
  },
  {
   "topic": "PCB pattern",
   "page": 26,
   "text": "Chips feel board flex and thermal stress more than leaded parts. Keep fillets low by sizing lands and dividing them from chassis, neighbouring leaded parts and wide copper with solder resist. A board whose expansion differs strongly from the chip, such as fluororesin or single-layer glass epoxy, can crack it. When moving to a smaller chip, revisit trace width, direction and copper thickness as well as the land."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Coating and cleaning",
   "page": 31,
   "text": "Choose low-shrinkage, low-moisture-absorption resins with expansion close to the chip's; a silicone undercoat can buffer stress, and epoxy absorbs little moisture. Avoid strongly acidic fluxes and coatings, and qualify the cleaning process so no flux residue is left."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Environment and safety",
   "page": 24,
   "text": "Do not run the part wet, in oil, sunlight, ozone, UV, radiation, toxic gas, condensation or beyond the vibration limits. A cracked part may short, so circuits where that could cause shock, smoke or fire need a fail-safe such as a fuse. The series carries no safety-standard certification. Waste goes to a licensed industrial disposal contractor."
  }
